# 0001 — Gate only new format and lint violations

- Status: proposed
- Date: <date the harness was adopted>

## Context
The harness was adopted into existing Swift code without reformatting it. Gating every file would fail on code
nobody touched, and reformatting it all at once is a large, unreviewed diff.

## What was tried
<Run `xcrun swift-format lint --strict` and `swiftlint lint --strict` over every tracked Swift file, with
`.swift-format` set to the code's existing style (indentation, line length), and record the counts here.>

## Evidence
<The warning counts above, and `.harness/swiftlint-baseline.json` (how many violations, which rules) as committed.>

## Limitations
- A file is formatted as a whole once it changes, so the first edit to an old file brings that file's full
  reformatting into the diff. The post-edit hook (`scripts/fix-file.sh`) formats the edited file in place.
- On `main` the base is `HEAD~1`, so a push of several direct commits checks only the last one's files.
- swift-format output can differ between versions; the CI Xcode pin and local toolchains may disagree.
- The SwiftLint baseline matches violations by file, rule and line content. Moving or renaming a file, or editing
  an offending line, can surface its old violation as new.
- SwiftLint relativises paths against Foundation's standardised current directory, which drops `/private` from
  `/private/tmp` and `/private/var`. The lint scripts pass absolute paths in that form (`scripts/standard-root.sh`)
  so the baseline matches in any checkout.

## Decision
- swift-format checks only Swift files changed since the merge-base with `main` (`origin/main` when there's no
  local `main`; `HARNESS_BASE_REF` overrides). On `main` itself it compares with `HEAD~1`, so direct commits are
  checked too. Committed, staged, unstaged and untracked files count. See `scripts/swift-quality.sh`.
- SwiftLint checks every Swift file with `--baseline .harness/swiftlint-baseline.json`, so only new violations fail.
  The owner writes the baseline once: `scripts/swift-quality.sh write-baseline`.
- Don't regenerate the baseline to make a change pass. Regenerate it only to shrink it after fixing old violations.

## Revisit if
- The whole codebase is reformatted in one deliberate change; then switch to gating every file and drop the baseline.
- The baseline is empty, or the changed-files check misses violations that reach `main`.
